1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,780
Welcome back.

2
00:00:01,040 --> 00:00:03,760
Now that we've completed our discussion of leukemias,

3
00:00:04,040 --> 00:00:05,440
we can now address lymph nodes.

4
00:00:05,920 --> 00:00:08,220
And the first topic that we're going to discuss

5
00:00:08,220 --> 00:00:09,780
is lymphadenopathy.

6
00:00:11,120 --> 00:00:13,860
Lymphadenopathy refers to enlarged lymph nodes.

7
00:00:14,820 --> 00:00:18,260
And it's important to note that lymphadenopathy

8
00:00:18,260 --> 00:00:20,180
can be painful or painless.

9
00:00:20,660 --> 00:00:22,720
Painful lymphadenopathy is usually seen

10
00:00:22,720 --> 00:00:24,000
when there's acute infection

11
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:26,620
and the lymph node is draining that region of infection.

12
00:00:27,140 --> 00:00:28,760
For example, let's say that the patient

13
00:00:28,760 --> 00:00:30,160
has a strep throat.

14
00:00:30,640 --> 00:00:33,380
The lymph nodes in the neck will then be enlarged

15
00:00:33,380 --> 00:00:34,560
and they will be painful.

16
00:00:34,960 --> 00:00:37,400
So that would be called painful lymphadenopathy.

17
00:00:38,060 --> 00:00:39,640
At the same time, it is also possible

18
00:00:39,640 --> 00:00:41,820
to develop a painless lymphadenopathy.

19
00:00:41,820 --> 00:00:43,700
And the differential diagnosis

20
00:00:43,700 --> 00:00:47,240
for painless lymphadenopathy includes chronic inflammation.

21
00:00:48,080 --> 00:00:49,980
So for example, autoimmune diseases

22
00:00:49,980 --> 00:00:53,700
often have painless lymphadenopathy associated with them.

23
00:00:54,360 --> 00:00:55,620
Metastatic carcinoma.

24
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:58,460
If carcinoma spreads to a local lymph node,

25
00:00:58,760 --> 00:01:02,100
it's usually painless enlargement of the lymph node that occurs.

26
00:01:02,500 --> 00:01:04,480
For example, if a woman has breast cancer

27
00:01:04,480 --> 00:01:06,780
and it spreads to the axillary lymph nodes,

28
00:01:07,080 --> 00:01:09,020
the axillary lymph nodes become enlarged

29
00:01:09,020 --> 00:01:10,340
and that's usually painless.

30
00:01:10,780 --> 00:01:13,360
And the third circumstance in which we can get

31
00:01:13,360 --> 00:01:15,380
painless lymphadenopathy is lymphoma.

32
00:01:16,220 --> 00:01:18,500
So when we think about painless lymphadenopathy,

33
00:01:18,640 --> 00:01:21,800
we really have three major differential diagnoses in our mind.

34
00:01:22,240 --> 00:01:26,100
Chronic inflammation, metastatic carcinoma, or lymphoma.

35
00:01:26,100 --> 00:01:30,340
We're going to cover lymphoma in much more detail in just a few slides.

36
00:01:30,760 --> 00:01:33,280
However, when we have a reactive lymphadenopathy,

37
00:01:33,720 --> 00:01:38,060
meaning that the lymph node is big due to some chronic inflammation or reactive process,

38
00:01:38,400 --> 00:01:42,120
it is important to note that there is going to be hyperplasia of differing

39
00:01:42,120 --> 00:01:45,660
regions of the lymph node depending on the underlying etiology.

40
00:01:46,420 --> 00:01:49,060
For example, if the patient has rheumatoid arthritis,

41
00:01:49,520 --> 00:01:52,060
it's classic for there to be expansion of the follicles

42
00:01:52,060 --> 00:01:55,960
and it's this expansion of the follicles that would lead to enlargement of the lymph node.

43
00:01:56,540 --> 00:02:00,480
Similarly, in early HIV, there's also expansion of the follicles

44
00:02:00,480 --> 00:02:05,360
and again, it's expansion of the follicles that results in the generalized lymphadenopathy

45
00:02:05,360 --> 00:02:07,900
that's often seen in early HIV.

46
00:02:08,640 --> 00:02:12,180
Now recall that HIV infects CD4-positive cells

47
00:02:12,180 --> 00:02:17,820
and of course, the most important and classic infection would be of the CD4-positive T cells.

48
00:02:18,260 --> 00:02:21,600
However, the cells in the follicle, the follicular dendritic cells,

49
00:02:21,940 --> 00:02:23,720
they also are CD4-positive.

50
00:02:24,140 --> 00:02:27,960
And that's why you see this follicular hyperplasia in early HIV.

51
00:02:28,940 --> 00:02:31,680
The paracortex is the region where the T cells live

52
00:02:31,680 --> 00:02:34,420
and so it's pretty straightforward for us to understand

53
00:02:34,420 --> 00:02:37,820
that with viral infection, the paracortex would become expanded

54
00:02:37,820 --> 00:02:41,580
and it's expansion of the paracortex that leads to lymphadenopathy.

55
00:02:41,960 --> 00:02:44,640
The classic example would be infectious mononucleosis.

56
00:02:45,080 --> 00:02:47,440
Recall that in infectious mononucleosis,

57
00:02:47,660 --> 00:02:52,200
an EBV infection results in enlargement of the lymph nodes

58
00:02:52,200 --> 00:02:55,900
and that's going to be due to paracortical expansion of the lymph node.

59
00:02:56,460 --> 00:02:59,000
And then finally, we can see enlargement of the lymph node

60
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:02,900
due to expansion of the region where the histiocytes live.

61
00:03:03,200 --> 00:03:05,400
And of course, the histiocytes live deep in the sinus

62
00:03:05,400 --> 00:03:06,780
in the medulla of the lymph node.

63
00:03:06,780 --> 00:03:09,660
And this is classically seen when there is a lymph node

64
00:03:09,660 --> 00:03:11,580
that is draining a region of cancer.

65
00:03:12,060 --> 00:03:13,760
Let's say that a woman has breast cancer

66
00:03:13,760 --> 00:03:17,000
and there are some lymph nodes that are draining that region.

67
00:03:17,420 --> 00:03:20,700
Well, it would be classic to see sinus histiocytosis

68
00:03:20,700 --> 00:03:21,800
in those lymph nodes.

69
00:03:21,980 --> 00:03:24,960
Now, of course, the cancer hasn't gone to the lymph node in this case,

70
00:03:25,220 --> 00:03:27,280
but this lymph node is simply becoming reactive

71
00:03:27,280 --> 00:03:30,040
because it's draining a region of cancer.

72
00:03:30,480 --> 00:03:33,800
And so that's another classic cause of lymphadenopathy.

73
00:03:34,560 --> 00:03:35,960
Here's a picture of a lymph node,

74
00:03:35,960 --> 00:03:38,740
just to remind ourselves of the normal architecture.

75
00:03:38,940 --> 00:03:41,280
Up here would be the cortex.

76
00:03:42,540 --> 00:03:44,620
Underneath the cortex is the paracortex

77
00:03:44,620 --> 00:03:46,780
and in this region is the medulla.

78
00:03:47,260 --> 00:03:49,620
Now, of course, the cortex is where the B cells live.

79
00:03:50,080 --> 00:03:52,420
The paracortex is where the T cells live.

80
00:03:52,660 --> 00:03:55,640
And the medulla, can you see these lighter pink areas here?

81
00:03:55,900 --> 00:03:58,600
This is where the sinus histiocytes live.

82
00:03:59,100 --> 00:04:01,280
And so these are the three major regions of the lymph node.

83
00:04:01,280 --> 00:04:04,920
Of course, we'd see follicular expansion in the cortex

84
00:04:04,920 --> 00:04:07,540
with rheumatoid arthritis and early HIV.

85
00:04:08,100 --> 00:04:11,280
We'd see expansion of the paracortex with viral infections.

86
00:04:11,840 --> 00:04:14,780
And again, we would see expansion of the medullary region

87
00:04:14,780 --> 00:04:19,100
when we had a lymph node that was draining a region of cancer.

88
00:04:19,100 --> 00:04:22,620
Now, I put this here in your text and in this discussion

89
00:04:22,620 --> 00:04:26,400
because it's often that examiners like to ask

90
00:04:26,400 --> 00:04:28,900
about which region of the lymph node is expanded

91
00:04:28,900 --> 00:04:31,300
based on a particular etiology.

92
00:04:31,960 --> 00:04:34,100
So that's a relatively short section.

93
00:04:34,200 --> 00:04:36,320
And now we're going to move into lymphoma.

94
00:04:36,680 --> 00:04:40,140
And basically, the very first principle that I'd like to establish

95
00:04:40,140 --> 00:04:44,080
is that lymphoma is a neoplastic proliferation of lymphoid cells

96
00:04:44,080 --> 00:04:47,940
that forms a mass, lymph, i.e. lymphoid cells,

97
00:04:48,420 --> 00:04:50,140
oma that forms a mass.

98
00:04:50,320 --> 00:04:53,180
And that's what gives it its name of lymphoma.

99
00:04:53,860 --> 00:04:56,620
Now, lymphoma can arise within the lymph node.

100
00:04:56,680 --> 00:04:59,480
And that makes sense because that's where lymphocytes normally live.

101
00:04:59,880 --> 00:05:02,960
But it can also arise within extranodal tissues

102
00:05:02,960 --> 00:05:04,900
outside of the lymph node.

103
00:05:05,460 --> 00:05:07,540
When we think about lymphoma generally,

104
00:05:07,900 --> 00:05:10,360
we should remind ourselves that lymphoma can be divided

105
00:05:10,360 --> 00:05:14,100
into non-Hodgkin's lymphoma, which is the more common type,

106
00:05:14,440 --> 00:05:17,860
and Hodgkin's lymphoma, which is still relatively common.

107
00:05:18,380 --> 00:05:21,160
We will talk in more detail about Hodgkin's lymphoma

108
00:05:21,160 --> 00:05:22,400
a little later on.

109
00:05:22,780 --> 00:05:24,320
And so at this point, what I'm going to do

110
00:05:24,320 --> 00:05:27,120
is simply focus on the non-Hodgkin's lymphomas.

111
00:05:27,960 --> 00:05:29,860
When we think about the non-Hodgkin's lymphoma,

112
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:32,940
we can classify them based on several parameters.

113
00:05:33,480 --> 00:05:36,820
These include cell type, for example, B cell versus T cell.

114
00:05:37,280 --> 00:05:40,340
Cell size, for example, small cells or intermediate,

115
00:05:40,360 --> 00:05:41,680
B cells or large cells.

116
00:05:42,320 --> 00:05:44,000
Pattern of growth, for example,

117
00:05:44,140 --> 00:05:46,840
do the cells grow in a diffuse sheet

118
00:05:46,840 --> 00:05:49,580
or do they form follicles, just as an example.

119
00:05:50,160 --> 00:05:53,380
Expression of surface markers, in particular,

120
00:05:53,560 --> 00:05:54,780
which would help us to determine

121
00:05:54,780 --> 00:05:56,980
whether the cells are B cells or T cells.

122
00:05:57,400 --> 00:05:59,660
And also cytogenetic translocations.

123
00:06:00,460 --> 00:06:03,080
Now, classically, when you think about non-Hodgkin's lymphoma,

124
00:06:03,460 --> 00:06:05,760
there have been multiple classification schemes.

125
00:06:05,880 --> 00:06:09,320
And I don't want to go into the nitty-gritty in details,

126
00:06:09,320 --> 00:06:11,860
but I'll just simply tell you that

127
00:06:11,860 --> 00:06:13,960
I think about Hodgkin's lymphoma

128
00:06:13,960 --> 00:06:16,080
in a rather simplistic manner.

129
00:06:16,660 --> 00:06:19,160
It's probably not the most accurate way

130
00:06:19,160 --> 00:06:22,220
if you were a hematopathologist or a hematologist,

131
00:06:22,380 --> 00:06:25,120
but I do think that it is an easy way

132
00:06:25,120 --> 00:06:28,160
to understand a very difficult concept.

133
00:06:28,760 --> 00:06:29,840
And so what we're going to do is

134
00:06:29,840 --> 00:06:32,340
I'm going to sort of base my division

135
00:06:32,340 --> 00:06:36,020
of non-Hodgkin's lymphoma on cell size.

136
00:06:36,020 --> 00:06:39,580
Now, in our discussion, we're going to be focusing

137
00:06:39,580 --> 00:06:42,360
on the B cell types of non-Hodgkin's lymphoma,

138
00:06:42,480 --> 00:06:45,320
which are the vast majority of non-Hodgkin's lymphoma.

139
00:06:45,640 --> 00:06:47,420
So we're going to base it on cell size,

140
00:06:47,820 --> 00:06:50,300
and part of that discussion will also be based

141
00:06:50,300 --> 00:06:51,620
on pattern of growth.

142
00:06:51,680 --> 00:06:53,680
But this will become clear just in a moment.

143
00:06:54,200 --> 00:06:56,720
So let's go to a whiteboard and remind ourselves

144
00:06:56,720 --> 00:06:59,380
that really when you think about cell size,

145
00:06:59,800 --> 00:07:01,440
lymphocytes, when they're neoplastic,

146
00:07:01,600 --> 00:07:05,060
can be small, can be intermediate, or can be large.

147
00:07:06,020 --> 00:07:07,240
When the lymphocytes are small,

148
00:07:07,420 --> 00:07:09,700
we would think about those as well-differentiated.

149
00:07:10,100 --> 00:07:12,720
In other words, small lymphocytes that are neoplastic

150
00:07:12,720 --> 00:07:14,280
are well-differentiated.

151
00:07:14,380 --> 00:07:18,400
They look just like very similar to normal lymphocytes.

152
00:07:18,960 --> 00:07:21,960
So they're going to do, on occasion, when they grow,

153
00:07:22,080 --> 00:07:23,800
they're going to grow in the patterns

154
00:07:23,800 --> 00:07:25,280
in which normal lymphocytes grow.

155
00:07:25,960 --> 00:07:27,840
Let's go back and remind ourselves

156
00:07:27,840 --> 00:07:29,460
of the normal lymph node.

157
00:07:29,920 --> 00:07:31,720
Remember that we're focusing on B cells,

158
00:07:31,820 --> 00:07:33,780
and so the normal B area of the lymph node

159
00:07:33,780 --> 00:07:35,820
is the cortex, and in the cortex,

160
00:07:36,040 --> 00:07:37,560
you have something called the follicle.

161
00:07:38,220 --> 00:07:39,140
That's the follicle.

162
00:07:39,940 --> 00:07:43,020
And around the follicle is the mantle.

163
00:07:43,860 --> 00:07:46,180
It's a dark area that surrounds the follicle.

164
00:07:46,620 --> 00:07:47,840
This is called the mantle.

165
00:07:51,620 --> 00:07:54,560
And then, outside of the mantle, on occasion,

166
00:07:55,420 --> 00:07:57,000
not always, but on occasion,

167
00:07:57,240 --> 00:07:58,780
we'll actually have generation

168
00:07:58,780 --> 00:08:00,240
of something called the margin.

169
00:08:00,240 --> 00:08:06,000
So these are the normal zones of the B area

170
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:09,820
of the lymph node, follicle, mantle, and margin.

171
00:08:10,280 --> 00:08:13,560
And when we think about cells that are small,

172
00:08:13,860 --> 00:08:14,900
that are neoplastic,

173
00:08:15,300 --> 00:08:17,260
they basically produce structures

174
00:08:17,260 --> 00:08:19,500
that are very similar to the normal lymph node.

175
00:08:19,900 --> 00:08:22,000
So we can have a follicular lymphoma

176
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:24,880
that's going to produce a bunch of follicle-like structures.

177
00:08:25,380 --> 00:08:28,380
We can have expansion of the mantle zone

178
00:08:28,380 --> 00:08:29,760
by neoplastic cells,

179
00:08:29,760 --> 00:08:31,780
which is called a mantle cell lymphoma.

180
00:08:32,180 --> 00:08:35,320
Or we can have expansion of the marginal zone

181
00:08:35,320 --> 00:08:36,780
by neoplastic cells,

182
00:08:37,120 --> 00:08:39,120
which is called a marginal zone lymphoma.

183
00:08:39,600 --> 00:08:41,920
And these are the three key lymphomas

184
00:08:41,920 --> 00:08:46,140
that arise from small neoplastic B cells.

185
00:08:46,540 --> 00:08:47,500
There is a fourth one,

186
00:08:47,560 --> 00:08:49,020
which I'm not going to discuss here,

187
00:08:49,260 --> 00:08:50,380
which we've already discussed,

188
00:08:50,500 --> 00:08:52,240
and that's small lymphocytic lymphoma.

189
00:08:52,340 --> 00:08:53,580
And recall that that is when

190
00:08:53,580 --> 00:08:55,820
CLL involves the lymph nodes.

191
00:08:56,260 --> 00:08:57,920
But basically, here I'm going to focus

192
00:08:57,920 --> 00:09:00,780
on follicular lymphoma, mantle cell lymphoma,

193
00:09:00,980 --> 00:09:02,420
and marginal zone lymphoma.

194
00:09:03,300 --> 00:09:05,920
Follicular lymphoma is a neoplastic proliferation

195
00:09:05,920 --> 00:09:07,160
of small B cells.

196
00:09:07,320 --> 00:09:09,240
Those B cells would be CD20 positive.

197
00:09:09,380 --> 00:09:11,340
CD20 is a key marker for B cells.

198
00:09:11,880 --> 00:09:13,420
And these small B cells are going to make

199
00:09:13,420 --> 00:09:14,720
follicle-like nodules,

200
00:09:14,840 --> 00:09:16,400
hence the name follicular lymphoma.

201
00:09:17,040 --> 00:09:18,860
Clinically, it presents in late adulthood

202
00:09:18,860 --> 00:09:21,960
and usually with painless lymphadenopathy.

203
00:09:22,580 --> 00:09:24,820
Now, lymphadenopathy refers to

204
00:09:24,820 --> 00:09:26,720
enlargement of the lymph nodes.

205
00:09:26,720 --> 00:09:28,540
And these patients will have generalized

206
00:09:28,540 --> 00:09:30,460
lymphadenopathy throughout the body

207
00:09:30,460 --> 00:09:31,780
that is painless.

208
00:09:32,500 --> 00:09:35,060
Here is a picture of follicular lymphoma.

209
00:09:35,500 --> 00:09:37,120
And what we can appreciate here

210
00:09:37,120 --> 00:09:38,700
is that we're dealing with a lymph node.

211
00:09:39,020 --> 00:09:40,360
And we see that the lymph node

212
00:09:40,360 --> 00:09:44,440
basically contains dozens of these follicles.

213
00:09:44,960 --> 00:09:48,260
So these lymphocytes are making follicle-like structures,

214
00:09:48,520 --> 00:09:50,320
hence the term follicular lymphoma.

215
00:09:50,840 --> 00:09:53,480
One of the important findings in follicular lymphoma

216
00:09:53,480 --> 00:09:58,480
is that it is driven by a translocation of 1418.

217
00:09:59,100 --> 00:10:01,620
Important to remember and very high yield

218
00:10:01,620 --> 00:10:03,140
that what's happening here

219
00:10:03,140 --> 00:10:05,020
is that if this is 14,

220
00:10:05,660 --> 00:10:06,960
normally in a B cell,

221
00:10:07,360 --> 00:10:09,640
on chromosome 14 at this particular locus

222
00:10:09,640 --> 00:10:11,320
is immunoglobulin heavy chain.

223
00:10:11,320 --> 00:10:14,900
And what translocates with immunoglobulin heavy chain

224
00:10:14,900 --> 00:10:16,380
in follicular lymphoma

225
00:10:16,380 --> 00:10:18,840
is a gene called BCL2.

226
00:10:19,460 --> 00:10:21,640
BCL2 happens to be on chromosome 18.

227
00:10:22,240 --> 00:10:24,500
And so this 1418 translocation

228
00:10:24,500 --> 00:10:27,580
pushes BCL2 into the position

229
00:10:27,580 --> 00:10:30,100
where immunoglobulin heavy chain was once sitting.

230
00:10:30,620 --> 00:10:32,160
Now, immunoglobulin heavy chain gene

231
00:10:32,160 --> 00:10:34,400
is a highly active gene in B cells.

232
00:10:34,820 --> 00:10:37,420
And so BCL2, when it gets translocated here,

233
00:10:37,820 --> 00:10:40,460
becomes highly active and becomes overexpressed.

234
00:10:41,320 --> 00:10:43,100
How does that create a follicular lymphoma?

235
00:10:43,980 --> 00:10:46,360
Let's recall that BCL2,

236
00:10:46,740 --> 00:10:49,900
its role is to stabilize the mitochondrial membrane.

237
00:10:50,260 --> 00:10:52,500
And by stabilizing the mitochondrial membrane,

238
00:10:52,720 --> 00:10:54,100
it prohibits cytochrome C

239
00:10:54,100 --> 00:10:55,960
from leaking from the mitochondria

240
00:10:55,960 --> 00:10:57,740
into the cytoplasm.

241
00:10:58,260 --> 00:11:00,840
And that blocks apoptosis.

242
00:11:01,380 --> 00:11:02,840
Most cells in the human body,

243
00:11:02,840 --> 00:11:04,260
they express BCL2

244
00:11:04,260 --> 00:11:06,600
because you don't want apoptosis to occur

245
00:11:06,600 --> 00:11:08,620
and you want to hide away cytochrome C.

246
00:11:08,620 --> 00:11:13,160
However, in the follicle of the lymph node,

247
00:11:13,760 --> 00:11:15,120
that's the one place,

248
00:11:15,640 --> 00:11:18,180
that's the particular place in the human body

249
00:11:18,180 --> 00:11:20,560
where you actually want apoptosis to occur.

250
00:11:20,820 --> 00:11:21,160
Why?

251
00:11:21,480 --> 00:11:23,160
Well, what's normally going on in the follicle?

252
00:11:23,260 --> 00:11:23,880
In the follicle,

253
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:26,220
you get somatic hypermutation of the B cells.

254
00:11:26,620 --> 00:11:27,700
The B cells come in

255
00:11:27,700 --> 00:11:30,320
and they are challenged by antigen

256
00:11:30,320 --> 00:11:32,460
and they undergo somatic hypermutation

257
00:11:32,460 --> 00:11:35,220
to see whether they can bind or produce antibodies

258
00:11:35,220 --> 00:11:37,540
that are going to bind against that antigen.

259
00:11:38,060 --> 00:11:40,100
If they pass somatic hypermutation,

260
00:11:40,580 --> 00:11:42,620
they then go from the germinal center

261
00:11:42,620 --> 00:11:45,660
and they can then participate in inflammation.

262
00:11:46,220 --> 00:11:49,260
However, if they fail somatic hypermutation,

263
00:11:49,560 --> 00:11:51,540
which the vast majority of B cells do,

264
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:53,600
then they undergo apoptosis.

265
00:11:53,780 --> 00:11:54,760
And when they undergo apoptosis,

266
00:11:55,460 --> 00:11:56,540
that allows them to die.

267
00:11:57,040 --> 00:11:59,540
Now imagine if there's a translocation of the B cells

268
00:11:59,540 --> 00:12:01,560
so that BCL2 is overexpressed,

269
00:12:01,960 --> 00:12:04,860
then those cells that should have died in the follicle

270
00:12:04,860 --> 00:12:06,660
actually won't be able to die.

271
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:08,780
And that's then going to result

272
00:12:08,780 --> 00:12:11,480
in a follicular lymphoma.

273
00:12:11,900 --> 00:12:15,240
So because we need apoptosis in the follicle

274
00:12:15,240 --> 00:12:18,800
and we aren't able to undergo apoptosis

275
00:12:18,800 --> 00:12:20,700
due to the high BCL2,

276
00:12:20,700 --> 00:12:23,220
a follicular lymphoma will develop.

277
00:12:24,100 --> 00:12:25,920
And that's what I'm highlighting here in your slide

278
00:12:25,920 --> 00:12:29,540
where I say that it's driven by a 14-18 translocation

279
00:12:29,540 --> 00:12:32,520
and that involves BCL2 on chromosome 18

280
00:12:32,520 --> 00:12:36,660
which translocates to the immunoglobulin heavy chain locus

281
00:12:36,660 --> 00:12:37,640
on chromosome 14.

282
00:12:38,120 --> 00:12:40,200
Again, overexpression of BCL2

283
00:12:40,200 --> 00:12:42,040
then inhibits apoptosis.

284
00:12:43,220 --> 00:12:45,580
These patients are relatively asymptomatic

285
00:12:45,580 --> 00:12:47,540
and they live for a long period of time

286
00:12:47,540 --> 00:12:48,340
with this disease.

287
00:12:48,840 --> 00:12:50,680
Treatment is usually reserved for patients

288
00:12:50,680 --> 00:12:51,660
who are symptomatic

289
00:12:51,660 --> 00:12:53,840
and one way by which we could treat

290
00:12:53,840 --> 00:12:55,320
is to give low-dose chemotherapy

291
00:12:55,320 --> 00:12:57,320
another way would be to give

292
00:12:57,320 --> 00:12:58,920
a drug called rituximab

293
00:12:58,920 --> 00:13:02,020
which is an anti-CD20 antibody.

294
00:13:02,840 --> 00:13:03,800
So this is another way

295
00:13:03,800 --> 00:13:06,260
by which we could treat follicular lymphoma.

296
00:13:07,140 --> 00:13:09,260
The complication of follicular lymphoma

297
00:13:09,260 --> 00:13:10,500
is that it can progress

298
00:13:10,500 --> 00:13:12,400
to a diffuse large B-cell lymphoma.

299
00:13:12,740 --> 00:13:13,540
So over time

300
00:13:13,540 --> 00:13:16,060
it could actually pick up additional mutations

301
00:13:16,060 --> 00:13:18,760
resulting in a diffuse large B-cell lymphoma

302
00:13:18,760 --> 00:13:21,380
and this would present as an enlarging lymph node.

303
00:13:21,780 --> 00:13:23,940
Now recall that patients with follicular lymphoma

304
00:13:23,940 --> 00:13:25,860
they have generalized lymphadenopathy

305
00:13:25,860 --> 00:13:27,480
where they have multiple lymph nodes

306
00:13:27,480 --> 00:13:28,840
in their body that are enlarged.

307
00:13:29,200 --> 00:13:30,300
If all of a sudden

308
00:13:30,300 --> 00:13:31,980
one of those lymph nodes

309
00:13:31,980 --> 00:13:34,120
begins to become further enlarged,

310
00:13:34,220 --> 00:13:35,260
begins to grow more,

311
00:13:35,540 --> 00:13:37,240
then we think about progression

312
00:13:37,240 --> 00:13:39,160
to diffuse large B-cell lymphoma.

313
00:13:39,820 --> 00:13:41,040
It is very important

314
00:13:41,040 --> 00:13:42,640
for the purposes of both clinics

315
00:13:42,640 --> 00:13:44,000
as well as board exams

316
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:46,840
to distinguish follicular hyperplasia

317
00:13:46,840 --> 00:13:48,340
from follicular lymphoma.

318
00:13:48,640 --> 00:13:49,780
Now what do I mean by that?

319
00:13:49,780 --> 00:13:51,740
If a patient gets an infection

320
00:13:51,740 --> 00:13:53,260
it is possible

321
00:13:53,260 --> 00:13:55,580
that they could get hyperplasia

322
00:13:55,580 --> 00:13:56,780
of the follicles

323
00:13:56,780 --> 00:13:59,200
just as a result of that infection.

324
00:14:00,480 --> 00:14:01,800
And so we need to

325
00:14:01,800 --> 00:14:03,860
when we see a bunch of follicles

326
00:14:03,860 --> 00:14:04,780
within a lymph node

327
00:14:04,780 --> 00:14:06,480
we need to be able to distinguish

328
00:14:06,480 --> 00:14:09,460
is this due to follicular lymphoma

329
00:14:09,460 --> 00:14:10,540
which is neoplastic

330
00:14:10,540 --> 00:14:12,420
or is this simply a reaction

331
00:14:12,420 --> 00:14:13,320
to an infection?

332
00:14:13,800 --> 00:14:15,260
Now how could we do that?

333
00:14:15,620 --> 00:14:16,400
Well number one

334
00:14:16,400 --> 00:14:18,360
when we have follicular lymphoma

335
00:14:18,360 --> 00:14:19,500
there will be disruption

336
00:14:19,500 --> 00:14:20,960
of the lymph node architecture.

337
00:14:21,800 --> 00:14:23,240
What do I mean by that?

338
00:14:23,300 --> 00:14:24,180
Well I'm going to take you back

339
00:14:24,180 --> 00:14:25,000
a couple slides

340
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:26,060
to this picture

341
00:14:26,060 --> 00:14:27,320
which I had drawn you here

342
00:14:27,320 --> 00:14:29,100
and let's look at this lymph node again.

343
00:14:29,540 --> 00:14:30,800
When we look at this lymph node

344
00:14:30,800 --> 00:14:31,500
what we see

345
00:14:31,500 --> 00:14:33,740
is that there are follicles

346
00:14:33,740 --> 00:14:35,740
throughout the entire lymph node

347
00:14:35,740 --> 00:14:37,800
and we don't see any other structures.

348
00:14:38,360 --> 00:14:39,780
So these follicles

349
00:14:39,780 --> 00:14:41,440
have basically taken over

350
00:14:41,440 --> 00:14:42,320
or disrupted

351
00:14:42,320 --> 00:14:43,980
the normal lymph node architecture

352
00:14:43,980 --> 00:14:45,400
and that's an indication

353
00:14:45,400 --> 00:14:46,020
that we're dealing

354
00:14:46,020 --> 00:14:47,200
with follicular lymphoma.

355
00:14:47,200 --> 00:14:49,780
If this was a follicular hyperplasia

356
00:14:49,780 --> 00:14:51,360
in response to an infection

357
00:14:51,360 --> 00:14:52,800
we would see expansion

358
00:14:52,800 --> 00:14:53,520
of the follicles

359
00:14:53,520 --> 00:14:54,740
but all of the follicles

360
00:14:54,740 --> 00:14:55,560
would be present

361
00:14:55,560 --> 00:14:56,460
in the cortex

362
00:14:56,460 --> 00:14:57,420
where the follicles

363
00:14:57,420 --> 00:14:58,260
normally live.

364
00:14:58,640 --> 00:14:59,880
The fact that these follicles

365
00:14:59,880 --> 00:15:01,440
are destroying the lymph node

366
00:15:01,440 --> 00:15:02,200
and going deep

367
00:15:02,200 --> 00:15:03,100
into the lymph node

368
00:15:03,100 --> 00:15:04,220
is an indication

369
00:15:04,220 --> 00:15:05,340
that we may be dealing

370
00:15:05,340 --> 00:15:07,080
with a follicular lymphoma.

371
00:15:07,500 --> 00:15:08,880
The second important feature

372
00:15:08,880 --> 00:15:10,740
concerning the distinction

373
00:15:10,740 --> 00:15:12,300
between hyperplasia

374
00:15:12,300 --> 00:15:13,000
and lymphoma

375
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:15,360
is a lack of tingible body macrophages

376
00:15:15,360 --> 00:15:16,360
in the germinal center.

377
00:15:17,100 --> 00:15:18,220
This image here

378
00:15:18,220 --> 00:15:19,440
is meant to highlight that.

379
00:15:19,980 --> 00:15:20,960
Now let's remind ourselves

380
00:15:20,960 --> 00:15:23,160
that when we have infection

381
00:15:23,160 --> 00:15:25,520
then we're going to get

382
00:15:25,520 --> 00:15:26,960
a follicular hyperplasia

383
00:15:26,960 --> 00:15:28,860
and that follicular hyperplasia

384
00:15:28,860 --> 00:15:30,480
occurs because B cells

385
00:15:30,480 --> 00:15:31,860
are coming into the follicle

386
00:15:31,860 --> 00:15:33,100
they are undergoing

387
00:15:33,100 --> 00:15:34,400
somatic hypermutation

388
00:15:34,400 --> 00:15:35,240
in the follicle

389
00:15:35,240 --> 00:15:36,700
and as a result

390
00:15:36,700 --> 00:15:37,900
of somatic hypermutation

391
00:15:37,900 --> 00:15:39,160
many of them are dying.

392
00:15:39,660 --> 00:15:40,860
Now if the B cells

393
00:15:40,860 --> 00:15:41,840
in the follicle

394
00:15:41,840 --> 00:15:43,260
are truly undergoing

395
00:15:43,260 --> 00:15:44,440
somatic hypermutation

396
00:15:44,440 --> 00:15:46,520
and dying via apoptosis

397
00:15:46,520 --> 00:15:47,580
then recall

398
00:15:47,580 --> 00:15:48,740
that the response

399
00:15:48,740 --> 00:15:49,660
to apoptosis

400
00:15:49,660 --> 00:15:50,640
at the very end

401
00:15:50,640 --> 00:15:51,820
is that the macrophages

402
00:15:51,820 --> 00:15:52,360
come in

403
00:15:52,360 --> 00:15:53,160
and they clean up

404
00:15:53,160 --> 00:15:53,980
the dead cells.

405
00:15:54,600 --> 00:15:55,500
And so if we have

406
00:15:55,500 --> 00:15:56,240
a follicle

407
00:15:56,240 --> 00:15:57,160
that is reacting

408
00:15:57,160 --> 00:15:57,960
to infection

409
00:15:57,960 --> 00:15:59,060
we should see

410
00:15:59,060 --> 00:16:00,480
a ton of macrophages

411
00:16:00,480 --> 00:16:01,560
present within

412
00:16:01,560 --> 00:16:02,360
the germinal center.

413
00:16:02,760 --> 00:16:03,320
And that's what

414
00:16:03,320 --> 00:16:04,700
these little white spaces

415
00:16:04,700 --> 00:16:05,580
here represent

416
00:16:05,580 --> 00:16:06,940
within this germinal center.

417
00:16:07,340 --> 00:16:08,220
So why don't I take you

418
00:16:08,220 --> 00:16:08,840
back a step

419
00:16:08,840 --> 00:16:09,580
and show you

420
00:16:09,580 --> 00:16:10,140
that this is

421
00:16:10,140 --> 00:16:10,960
the germinal center.

422
00:16:11,420 --> 00:16:12,520
This darker blue area

423
00:16:12,520 --> 00:16:13,400
here is actually

424
00:16:13,400 --> 00:16:13,980
the mantle

425
00:16:13,980 --> 00:16:15,400
surrounding the germinal center

426
00:16:15,400 --> 00:16:16,980
and within this germinal center

427
00:16:16,980 --> 00:16:18,720
we see these white spaces

428
00:16:18,720 --> 00:16:19,500
which indicate

429
00:16:19,500 --> 00:16:20,540
that macrophages

430
00:16:20,540 --> 00:16:21,500
are present

431
00:16:21,500 --> 00:16:22,260
and are eating

432
00:16:22,260 --> 00:16:23,560
the debris

433
00:16:23,560 --> 00:16:24,880
of apoptotic cells.

434
00:16:25,400 --> 00:16:26,220
These macrophages

435
00:16:26,220 --> 00:16:27,040
are also called

436
00:16:27,040 --> 00:16:29,320
tingible body macrophages

437
00:16:29,320 --> 00:16:30,520
and they indicate

438
00:16:30,520 --> 00:16:30,960
again

439
00:16:30,960 --> 00:16:32,380
that apoptosis

440
00:16:32,380 --> 00:16:33,700
is actively occurring

441
00:16:33,700 --> 00:16:34,820
within the germinal center.

442
00:16:35,280 --> 00:16:36,140
Now let's contrast

443
00:16:36,140 --> 00:16:36,680
that against

444
00:16:36,680 --> 00:16:37,640
follicular lymphoma.

445
00:16:37,800 --> 00:16:38,680
Here's the follicle

446
00:16:38,680 --> 00:16:39,860
of follicular lymphoma.

447
00:16:40,140 --> 00:16:40,860
Do you see

448
00:16:40,860 --> 00:16:42,260
any tingible body macrophages

449
00:16:42,260 --> 00:16:43,100
in that follicle?

450
00:16:43,560 --> 00:16:44,480
The answer is no.

451
00:16:44,720 --> 00:16:45,620
We don't have

452
00:16:45,620 --> 00:16:46,240
those nice

453
00:16:46,240 --> 00:16:47,540
tingible body macrophages

454
00:16:47,540 --> 00:16:48,100
in the center

455
00:16:48,100 --> 00:16:48,800
of that follicle

456
00:16:48,800 --> 00:16:50,200
and that's because

457
00:16:50,200 --> 00:16:50,960
these cells

458
00:16:50,960 --> 00:16:51,960
are not undergoing

459
00:16:51,960 --> 00:16:52,800
apoptosis.

460
00:16:53,360 --> 00:16:54,140
They have undergone

461
00:16:54,140 --> 00:16:55,920
a 14-18 translocation.

462
00:16:56,400 --> 00:16:58,100
BCL2 is overexpressed

463
00:16:58,100 --> 00:16:59,440
and the overexpression

464
00:16:59,440 --> 00:17:00,180
of BCL2

465
00:17:00,180 --> 00:17:01,440
is actually blocking

466
00:17:01,440 --> 00:17:02,280
apoptosis

467
00:17:02,280 --> 00:17:03,260
in these cells.

468
00:17:03,820 --> 00:17:04,700
And that's an important

469
00:17:04,700 --> 00:17:05,720
point concerning

470
00:17:05,720 --> 00:17:06,800
follicular lymphoma.

471
00:17:07,440 --> 00:17:08,540
A third important point

472
00:17:08,540 --> 00:17:09,820
concerning follicular lymphoma

473
00:17:09,820 --> 00:17:10,680
is the expression

474
00:17:10,680 --> 00:17:11,540
of BCL2

475
00:17:11,540 --> 00:17:12,320
in the follicles.

476
00:17:12,320 --> 00:17:13,640
The vast majority

477
00:17:13,640 --> 00:17:14,080
of cells

478
00:17:14,080 --> 00:17:14,840
in the human body

479
00:17:14,840 --> 00:17:16,280
they express BCL2

480
00:17:16,280 --> 00:17:17,340
and that's because

481
00:17:17,340 --> 00:17:18,140
you don't want

482
00:17:18,140 --> 00:17:18,720
those cells

483
00:17:18,720 --> 00:17:20,080
to undergo apoptosis.

484
00:17:20,600 --> 00:17:20,920
However,

485
00:17:21,100 --> 00:17:21,900
in the follicle,

486
00:17:22,460 --> 00:17:23,660
in a normal follicle

487
00:17:23,660 --> 00:17:24,640
in you and I

488
00:17:24,640 --> 00:17:25,700
there would be

489
00:17:25,700 --> 00:17:26,440
no expression

490
00:17:26,440 --> 00:17:27,400
of BCL2

491
00:17:27,400 --> 00:17:28,960
because you want

492
00:17:28,960 --> 00:17:30,380
to turn BCL2 off

493
00:17:30,380 --> 00:17:31,200
to allow

494
00:17:31,200 --> 00:17:32,320
for apoptosis.

495
00:17:33,020 --> 00:17:33,740
But if there is

496
00:17:33,740 --> 00:17:34,960
expression of BCL2

497
00:17:34,960 --> 00:17:35,820
that would indicate

498
00:17:35,820 --> 00:17:36,640
that we're dealing

499
00:17:36,640 --> 00:17:37,980
with a follicular lymphoma.

500
00:17:38,480 --> 00:17:39,180
The final way

501
00:17:39,180 --> 00:17:39,920
by which we can make

502
00:17:39,920 --> 00:17:40,540
this distinction

503
00:17:40,540 --> 00:17:41,720
is monoclonality.

504
00:17:42,220 --> 00:17:43,540
Recall that if we have

505
00:17:43,540 --> 00:17:44,740
a reactive proliferation

506
00:17:44,740 --> 00:17:45,480
of lymphocytes

507
00:17:45,480 --> 00:17:46,200
and this goes back

508
00:17:46,200 --> 00:17:47,320
to our discussions

509
00:17:47,320 --> 00:17:48,240
of neoplasia,

510
00:17:48,640 --> 00:17:49,560
if you have a reactive

511
00:17:49,560 --> 00:17:50,920
proliferation of lymphocytes

512
00:17:50,920 --> 00:17:51,660
it would be

513
00:17:51,660 --> 00:17:52,300
polyclonal.

514
00:17:52,820 --> 00:17:53,420
And if we have

515
00:17:53,420 --> 00:17:54,780
a neoplastic proliferation

516
00:17:54,780 --> 00:17:55,420
of lymphocytes

517
00:17:55,420 --> 00:17:57,160
it would be monoclonal.

518
00:17:57,460 --> 00:17:57,860
And of course

519
00:17:57,860 --> 00:17:59,160
clonality is gauged

520
00:17:59,160 --> 00:18:00,100
in B cells

521
00:18:00,100 --> 00:18:01,120
by looking at

522
00:18:01,120 --> 00:18:01,700
the ratio

523
00:18:01,700 --> 00:18:02,800
of kappa to lambda

524
00:18:02,800 --> 00:18:03,400
light chain.

525
00:18:03,820 --> 00:18:05,320
In a polyclonal

526
00:18:05,320 --> 00:18:06,800
reactive proliferation

527
00:18:06,800 --> 00:18:08,260
the ratio

528
00:18:08,260 --> 00:18:09,220
would be 3 to 1

529
00:18:09,220 --> 00:18:10,660
whereas in neoplasia

530
00:18:10,660 --> 00:18:11,160
the ratio

531
00:18:11,160 --> 00:18:11,940
would be disrupted

532
00:18:11,940 --> 00:18:12,700
for example

533
00:18:12,700 --> 00:18:13,360
classically

534
00:18:13,360 --> 00:18:14,260
on an examination

535
00:18:14,260 --> 00:18:15,220
20 to 1

536
00:18:15,220 --> 00:18:16,480
or 25 to 1.

537
00:18:17,040 --> 00:18:17,540
So that is

538
00:18:17,540 --> 00:18:19,000
follicular lymphoma.

539
00:18:19,400 --> 00:18:20,160
Now we come

540
00:18:20,160 --> 00:18:21,360
to mantle cell lymphoma

541
00:18:21,360 --> 00:18:22,540
and mantle cell lymphoma

542
00:18:22,540 --> 00:18:23,200
is when you have

543
00:18:23,200 --> 00:18:24,600
a neoplastic proliferation

544
00:18:24,600 --> 00:18:25,620
of small B cells

545
00:18:25,620 --> 00:18:27,300
obviously CD20 positive

546
00:18:27,300 --> 00:18:28,460
that expand

547
00:18:28,460 --> 00:18:29,360
the mantle zone.

548
00:18:29,800 --> 00:18:30,380
This clinically

549
00:18:30,380 --> 00:18:31,180
also presents

550
00:18:31,180 --> 00:18:31,920
in late adulthood

551
00:18:31,920 --> 00:18:32,900
and also presents

552
00:18:32,900 --> 00:18:33,560
with generalized

553
00:18:33,560 --> 00:18:35,340
painless lymphadenopathy.

554
00:18:36,160 --> 00:18:36,420
Now

555
00:18:36,420 --> 00:18:37,340
when you have

556
00:18:37,340 --> 00:18:38,560
this type of tumor

557
00:18:38,560 --> 00:18:39,400
one of the classic

558
00:18:39,400 --> 00:18:40,720
descriptions on examination

559
00:18:40,720 --> 00:18:42,420
is that the examiners

560
00:18:42,420 --> 00:18:43,060
could tell you

561
00:18:43,060 --> 00:18:43,920
that there is

562
00:18:43,920 --> 00:18:45,060
a neoplastic

563
00:18:45,060 --> 00:18:46,220
proliferation of cells

564
00:18:46,220 --> 00:18:47,420
that expands

565
00:18:47,420 --> 00:18:48,220
the region

566
00:18:48,220 --> 00:18:49,660
immediately adjacent

567
00:18:49,660 --> 00:18:50,500
to the follicle.

568
00:18:51,040 --> 00:18:51,460
If you have

569
00:18:51,460 --> 00:18:51,880
a tumor

570
00:18:51,880 --> 00:18:52,940
that expands

571
00:18:52,940 --> 00:18:53,920
the region

